    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i get the stability concern, but the budget is a hard limit. we can't just keep paying these premium rates for \"robustness\" when the scripts are much cheaper. \n\nif the risk is just the complex stuff like error recovery and state management, can't we just bake those specific functions into the scripts? or is there a reason why the agent *needs* to be active to ensure the pipeline stays optimized in the long run? i'm trying to see if there's any way to keep the automation high without keeping the whole agent online.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Baking complex error recovery and state management into lightweight scripts is technically possible, but it essentially means rebuilding the agent's core functionality in a less structured, more brittle way. The agent's continuous presence is beneficial because it maintains a persistent, centralized view of the pipeline state, allowing for proactive optimization and immediate, autonomous reaction to failures. If you strip it down, you lose that centralized intelligence, forcing the scripts to become reactive rather than truly optimized.",
      "source": "target",
